# gitleaks allowlist - owner: 03 Security
#
# RULE: one line per finding, each with a reason, each re-checked when the
# file changes. NEVER add a whole directory. A blanket "vendor/" ignore is
# how a real key pasted into a vendored file stops being noticed.
#
# 1) supabase-js, vendored and minified at a pinned version. The generic
#    rule matches the token sequence after `fetchWithAuth=` inside the
#    minified bundle. Verified by eye: it is library code, not a credential.
#    If this file is ever re-vendored at a new version, DELETE this line,
#    re-scan, and re-verify before adding it back.
vendor/supabase.js:generic-api-key:1

# 2) The Supabase PUBLISHABLE key in config.js, on both copies. This one is
#    meant to be in our JavaScript - the browser has to receive it, so hiding
#    it in a .env would be theatre. It is safe ONLY because row level security
#    is on, which is verified in evidence/se-m1-verify-2026-09-21.txt.
#
#    IMPORTANT - why this does not weaken us: the pre-commit hook
#    (tools/hooks/pre-commit, rule 1) still blocks any REAL JWT in this exact
#    file, deliberately, and its comment says not to add an exception for
#    config.js. So a service-role key pasted here by a tired teammate on
#    Tuesday is still caught - by the hook, at commit time, before it reaches
#    the history. gitleaks stops repeating a finding we have already judged;
#    the hook keeps guarding the thing that would actually hurt.
js/config.js:generic-api-key:16
app-retag/js/config.js:generic-api-key:16
